import{_ as t,o,c as a,a0 as n}from"./chunks/framework.ChlVJx0I.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"The diagnostics loop is the job, and v1 is read-only","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0040-the-diagnostics-loop-is-the-job-and-v1-is-read-only.md","filePath":"adr/0040-the-diagnostics-loop-is-the-job-and-v1-is-read-only.md","lastUpdated":1792093740000}'),s={name:"adr/0040-the-diagnostics-loop-is-the-job-and-v1-is-read-only.md"};function i(r,e,d,h,l,c){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="the-diagnostics-loop-is-the-job-and-v1-is-read-only" tabindex="-1">The diagnostics loop is the job, and v1 is read-only <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-diagnostics-loop-is-the-job-and-v1-is-read-only" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The diagnostics loop is the job, and v1 is read-only&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Issue #136 asks whether LSP tooling helps an agent at all. The market answer is navigation: every LSP bridge exposes go-to-definition, references, hover, and symbols, and this repo already has a code-intelligence extension that owns symbol search, file reading, and the call trail. Measurement says the navigation pitch is weak for agents: <code>ianbarber/lsps-for-llms</code> gave seven models LSP tools over a grep-and-read baseline and the models largely declined the operations they were offered; gains needed a prompt, training, or a gate. What shipped systems report as wins (Hermes Agent, LSPSteer, Kilocode) is the other shape: after an edit, the real language server reports what the edit broke, and the agent sees it in the same result. pi has no compiler-in-the-loop today.</p><p><strong>The extension&#39;s primary job is Post-edit diagnostics, and v1 is read-only.</strong> Navigation is taken only where a Language server clearly beats codegraph: exact references and the real type behind a symbol. Three tools exist (<code>lsp_diagnostics</code>, <code>lsp_references</code>, <code>lsp_hover</code>); there is no <code>lsp_symbols</code>, no <code>lsp_definition</code>, and no call hierarchy. The Post-edit annotation is attached in the <code>tool_result</code> hook on <code>edit</code> and <code>write</code>, so it reaches the model without the model deciding to ask, and the Sweep at turn end covers edits made outside pi&#39;s edit tools.</p><p><strong>The turn-end message never requests a continuation.</strong> <code>turn_end</code> may append a <code>custom_message</code> and ask for one more model request; this extension appends the message and returns nothing, so the agent reads it at the start of its next turn. An unconditional continuation can loop, and a diagnostics report is not a reason to start a turn by itself.</p><p><strong>Considered options.</strong> Navigation-first, like the MCP bridges, buys tools the agent will not call and duplicates codegraph. A diagnostics tool alone was rejected on the same measurement: an offered check is a check the agent can skip. Write operations (<code>textDocument/rename</code>, code actions, formatting) are deferred to the v2 ticket rather than cut forever: they make the Language server a writer in a session where the agent expects its own edit tools to be the only writer, and they collide with edit-assist, so they need their own design for confirmation and undo.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> The extension can be useful with all three tools inactive, because the annotation is a hook and not a tool. The prompt note appears only when a Language server is available for the project, so a repo with no server on the machine pays no context and is never steered at a tool that would fail. The boundary against codegraph is stated in the note and in the docs, because two code-intelligence extensions in one package otherwise compete for the same call.</p>',6)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
